          ],
          "notes": "The full report is at /Users/erwingarcia/Claude/JWFOODSPROPOSAL/research/competitors-global.md. It covers 22 benchmarks ranked best first, a cross-cutting playbook (subscription mechanics, B2B channels, AI use) and clearly labelled revenue estimates for J&W.\n\nKey local finding: JBS's Wild Fork is leaving Canada. Online service ended October 13, 2026, and its stores in Whitby, Oakville, Ancaster and Newmarket close by December 14, 2026 (Retail Insider). Three of those towns are on J&W's existing delivery routes, so there is an immediate chance to win those customers.\n\nTwo direct competitors already ship into Ontario: CookUnity (in Toronto since June 2025, with US$750M+ ARR) and Factor.\n\nMost of the global leaders run no visible AI features. The exceptions are Calo Black (an LLM private chef), Territory's ML regional menus, and Icon Meals' AI-search blog. That leaves room for J&W to stand out with an AI concierge.\n\nPrice check (estimate): J&W's cooked chicken works out to about C$16.80/lb ($4.20 per 4oz), roughly on par with Trifecta's bulk US$14.99/lb.\n\nResearch limits: the shared web-search budget ran out before everything was covered, so some figures are listed as not found. These are Omaha Steaks revenue, Huel, Thrive Market, Balance Meals, current Allen Brothers and Crowd Cow figures, per-lb bulk prices at Icon and MegaFit, and Factor-only revenue. Prep Kitchen's revenue figures conflict: £13.9M (Sunday Times 2024), £22.8M (FEBE 2024) and £40M+ (company claim). superfitfoods.com now redirects to a 'domain for sale' page, so that business appears inactive. Its gym-cooler model is still worth copying.\n\nThe revenue estimates for J&W in section 4 of the report are illustrative assumptions, not forecasts."
        }
