      {
        "name": "Icon Meals (Wholesale program), Dallas, Texas, USA",
        "verifiedOperating": true,
        "verificationNote": "Operating as of Oct 16, 2026. Evidence:\n- The Shopify catalogue was updated 2026-10-16, new products were published Aug 18 and Sep 4, 2026, and the footer reads © 2026.\n- The newest Trustpilot review (Oct 2, 2026) describes a live order. Instagram (157K followers) last posted Jul 21, 2026.\n- LinkedIn shows posts from about 2 months ago and a DoorDash launch about 4 months ago.\n\nIt is premium in positioning and price, but not in current execution:\n- Trustpilot gives a TrustScore of 1.7/5 from 980 reviews, and every review from May to Oct 2026 is 1-star. They cite weeks-long backlogs, food arriving thawed, unreachable support and a cancelled order that shipped anyway. The company does not reply.\n- Both blog feeds went quiet in Feb 2026.\n- The wholesale program is thin online. All three 'Apply Now' buttons link to the homepage, so there is no application form or published wholesale pricing. 'Wholesale Login' is the standard Shopify customer-account login.\n\nUse Icon as a positioning reference (plant, gyms, clinics, pro teams), not as a model of service or B2B mechanics.\n\nLimitation: the shared web-search budget ran out mid-task, so I could not search for 2026 news such as funding, layoffs or ownership changes. Operating status rests on direct fetches of Icon's live store, Trustpilot, LinkedIn and Instagram.",
        "premiumEvidence": "Premium signals:\n- The wholesale page shows logos for UFC, U.S. Olympic Team, Dallas Cowboys, USN, USA Triathlon and Absolute Recomp under 'The Official Meal Delivery Service for these brands' (company claim).\n- UFC named Icon its Official Meal Delivery Partner and took an equity stake on Sep 28, 2021, citing 56% annualised growth since its 2014 founding (ufc.com).\n- Revenue was 'over $14 million in 2023' with 75 employees (Garage Gym Reviews, updated May 13, 2026; third-party, unaudited). LinkedIn shows a 51-200 band.\n- Company claims: 'Over 12 million meals sold' and wholesale partners 'thousands across the United States', with orders from 'a few dozen meals to weekly shipments of thousands'.\n- It claims a '100% USDA-certified facility' with in-house meat processing (cooks its own ground turkey, hand-trims brisket). The how-it-works page instead says 'HACCP-certified kitchen'.\n- Signature meals cost US$12.20-19.20 (C$17-27).\n\nAgainst these, the current Trustpilot score is 1.7/5 (980 reviews), with only 1-star reviews in 2026.",
        "pricing": "USD prices (C$ at 1.4196, Bank of Canada 2026-10-15):\n\n- **Signature meals** (8 oz / 227 g trays): 25 priced meals at $12.20-19.20, median $13.40 (C$17.32-27.26, median C$19.02). Examples:\n  - Baja Pulled Chicken Bowl $12.40\n  - Diced Chicken, Rice & Green Beans $13.00\n  - Salmon, Saffron Rice & Green Beans $15.60\n  - Double Protein Saffron Rice $19.20\n- **Meal plans** (12 or 18 meals; there is no 24-meal plan now):\n  - ICON Meal Plan: $144 ($12.00/meal) or $209 ($11.61/meal)\n  - Keto and Get Lean: $138 ($11.50) or $205 ($11.39)\n  - Extreme Protein: $169 ($14.09) or $249 ($13.83)\n  - Subscribe & Save is 5%, delivered every 1-4 weeks (Recharge). Build Your Own has a 0% subscription discount.\n- **Bulk cooked 'by the pound'** (16 items; no unit shown on the page; 1 lb inferred from the handle and the 454 g Shopify weight):\n  - Chicken breast $15.20/lb (C$21.58), thighs $14.80, ground turkey $14.80, ground beef $19.20, brisket $23.80, salmon $27.60, steak $27.80, saffron rice $12.00.\n  - No quantity tiers.\n- **Custom meal builder:**\n  - Base $6.00, plus chicken $0.80/oz (3-8 oz adds $2.40-6.40), salmon $1.10-1.74/oz, steak $1.60/oz.\n  - Sides: white rice $1.48 per cup, broccoli $0.21/oz.\n  - Optional seasoning +$9.99.\n  - Example: 6 oz chicken + 1 cup rice + 4 oz broccoli = $13.12 (C$18.63).\n- **Policies and extras:**\n  - 8-meal minimum per shipment, site-wide (waived for pickup and select local partners). Ships frozen on dry ice; shipping fee not found.\n  - Sign-up 10% off; military and first responders 15% off.\n  - Gift cards $10-500. Protein popcorn and seasonings $9.99. Protein coffee $35.\n  - Wholesale price list: not published. A hidden $0 'Wholesale Payment' product suggests invoices are paid through the Shopify checkout.\n- **J&W comparison:**\n  - Cooked chicken: J&W C$16.72-19.96/lb vs Icon C$21.58/lb.\n  - Comparable custom bowl: J&W about C$10.00 vs Icon C$18.63, about 46% cheaper.\n  - Pre-set meals: J&W C$8.99-14.99 vs Icon C$17.32-27.26.",
        "scale": "- Revenue: over US$14M in 2023, with 75 employees (Garage Gym Reviews; third-party).\n- LinkedIn: 51-200 employee band, 15 on LinkedIn, HQ Farmers Branch TX, founded 2014. GGR says 2012, which conflicts.\n- Growth: 56% annualised since 2014, as of 2021 (UFC.com).\n- Funding: UFC equity stake (2021, terms undisclosed). US$7.09M raised per CB Insights (via competitors-global.md).\n- Company claims: over 12M meals sold; 'thousands' of wholesale partners.\n- Catalogue: 188 Shopify products, including 41 signature meals, 16 bulk items and 6 plans.\n- Social: Instagram 157K followers; LinkedIn 496.\n- Reviews: Trustpilot 980 reviews, TrustScore 1.7.\n- New channel: DoorDash launch of 10 meals in select cities (about mid-2026, per LinkedIn).\n- Tech stack in page source: Shopify, Recharge, Bold, Klaviyo, Yotpo, Searchanise.",
        "siteDesign": "- **Layout:**\n  - White sticky header: logo left, four uppercase nav items with chevrons (Order Meals / Snacks & More / How It Works / More), account and cart icons right.\n  - Full-bleed food hero with a dark overlay. Left-aligned white uppercase headline (about 56-64px), a small 'over 12 million meals sold' badge above it, and two pill CTAs below: solid red and white-outline.\n  - Sections alternate between a light-grey canvas and a black full-width band holding white rounded audience cards (portrait photo, uppercase title, red text link).\n  - Wholesale page, centred single column in this order:\n    1. White hero card\n    2. Three-icon 'Why Choose' strip\n    3. Colour partner-logo row\n    4. Three rounded red tiles with product cut-outs\n    5. Photo collage beside the segment copy\n    6. White text card over a full-bleed tray photo\n    7. Product grid\n  - Product cards: white, rounded, soft shadow. Each shows a tray photo, bold name, diet tags, price, a macro strip (Cals/Pro/Carbs/Fat) and a −/+ stepper.\n  - Builder pages use a two-step progress header ('1 Choose Meals → 2 Review Cart') and a sticky right-hand 'Review Your Order' cart.\n- **Palette** (CSS variables):\n  - Accent / CTA / links #ec4242; nav hover #d42828\n  - Page background #f5f5f5; cards and header #ffffff\n  - Body text #333333; headings and dark bands #000000 / #1a1a1a\n  - Footer #252525; muted #a5a5a5; borders #E5E7EB\n  - Button hover turns black with #f5f5f5 text.\n- **Typography:** Jost (geometric sans) throughout, 16px base. Headers uppercase, weight 500, widely tracked (40px section heads). Buttons 14px uppercase.\n- **Photography:** overhead and three-quarter shots of glossy, saturated meals in black prep trays on light marble or grey; lifestyle portraits (busy professional at a laptop, fitness couple); partner and staff photos; product cut-outs on red tiles.\n- **Tone:** punchy, gym-adjacent and cheeky, with lots of 'ICON/Iconic' wordplay and confident authority claims. Examples of the register: a hero playing on making clean eating consistent; audience cards like 'Fast Food Breakups' and 'Macro Pros'.\n- **For J&W:** keep the structure (proof wall → segments → plant story → shoppable macro grid) but use J&W's own colours, real plant and butcher photography, and its family-butcher voice. Do not copy the copy.",
        "keyFlows": [
          "WHOLESALE LANDING (/pages/wholesale), in this order: (1) hero 'Let's Start an Iconic Partnership' with an Apply button; (2) three-icon value strip; (3) partner logo wall (UFC, Olympic, Cowboys, USN, USAT, Absolute Recomp); (4) three product-category tiles (Heat & Eat / Protein Snacks / Sauces & Seasonings); (5) segment list (store, gym, weight-loss clinic, training center, office, employee meal benefit), with scale claims and a second CTA; (6) plant story (USDA facility, in-house meat processing); (7) functional-foods upsell with a third CTA; (8) 'Latest Arrivals' shoppable grid with macros. NOTE: all Apply CTAs link to the homepage, so the funnel is broken. J&W should copy the order and add a real application form, tiered case pricing and terms.",
          "CUSTOM MEAL BUILDER (/products/custom-meals): base price $6.00, which 'updates as you choose'. (1) Protein dropdown with 11 options; (2) portion bubbles from 3 to 8 oz, each showing its price (chicken $2.40-6.40); (3) Side 1 dropdown with 13 options and a portion choice; (4) Side 2 dropdown; (5) optional seasoning (+$9.99); (6) quantity stepper, then ADD. A sticky right-side cart and a two-step progress header ('1 Choose Meals → 2 Review Cart') stay in view. J&W already sells every component by the ounce, so it can build this directly.",
          "MEAL PLAN PURCHASE: (1) pick 12 or 18 meals, with the per-meal price in the variant label; (2) choose One-Time or Subscribe & Save (5%); (3) set delivery frequency to every 1-4 weeks; (4) Add to Cart. The cart drawer enforces an 8-meal minimum with 'Continue Shopping' or 'Checkout Anyway'. Build Your Own plan: 12 or 18 meals, every 1 or 2 weeks.",
          "HOMEPAGE AUDIENCE ROUTER: under 'Who is ICON Meals For?', four cards each send the visitor to a different path: I'm Too Busy → menu; Macro Pros → custom meals; GLP-1 Users; Fast Food Breakups. A 'Shop By Goal' row follows: Clean Variety / Build Muscle / KETO / Get Lean, each mapped to a plan.",
          "SHARE-CART / PRE-BUILT CART LINK: a 'Share Cart' button and a 'You have great friends... building your cart now!' loader let one person send a pre-filled cart URL. J&W could use this so trainers and partners send members a ready-made weekly order tied to their member code.",
          "GIFT FLOW: gift cards from $10 to $500 (two products, $25-500 and $10-500), delivered by email, 'no additional processing fees'.",
          "AI-SEARCH CONTENT: /blogs/ai-optimized holds question-titled, answer-first posts (GLP-1 picks, shelf life, keto guides), linked in the footer as an 'AI Knowledge Base'. The feed has been dormant since Feb 2026."
        ],
        "featuresToBorrow": [
          "A dedicated B2B landing page with a partner proof wall, a segment list (gyms, clinics, trainers, offices, employee meal benefits, retailers) and a plant-capability story. J&W should add the working application form, tiered case pricing and terms that Icon lacks.",
          "A segment-specific scale line ('from a few dozen meals to thousands weekly'). J&W's version: 'from one case to a weekly route drop'.",
          "A live-priced custom meal builder with per-ounce portion bubbles, two sides, a running price and a sticky cart.",
          "Goal-named plans (Get Lean / Lean Lifter / Extreme Protein / Keto), shown in 12- and 18-meal sizes with the per-meal price in the label and a 1-4 week subscription frequency.",
          "Macro strips (Cals / Protein / Carbs / Fat) and diet tags on every product card.",
          "A homepage audience router with a GLP-1 users card. This fits J&W's small-portion, salt- and sugar-free line.",
          "A bulk 'by the pound' cooked-protein collection. J&W should show the unit and add case tiers, which Icon does not.",
          "A share-cart link that trainers and partners can send to members.",
          "Question-titled, answer-first blog posts as an AI-search 'knowledge base'.",
          "Military and first-responder discount, adapted for J&W as a front-line, healthcare or police-and-fire program.",
          "A wholesale invoice-payment product, so B2B accounts can pay invoices through the same checkout."
        ],
        "whatJWDoesBetter": [
          "A provincially inspected plant (since 2008), a butcher-family heritage (Advance Food Products 1973, J&W 1995) and a 2026 federal chicken TRQ processor quota. These are verifiable credentials, set against Icon's self-described 'USDA-certified' kitchen, which its own how-it-works page calls a 'HACCP-certified kitchen'.",
          "Price: J&W's cooked chicken is C$16.72-19.96/lb against Icon's C$21.58/lb. A comparable custom bowl is about C$10.00 against C$18.63 (about 46% cheaper). Pre-set meals are C$8.99-14.99 against Icon signature meals at C$17.32-27.26.",
          "A stricter clean-recipe promise: every Meal Butler item is salt- and sugar-free with no added oils. Icon sells mac & cheese, ravioli, sliders and $9.99 flavoured seasonings.",
          "True à-la-carte cooked portions by the ounce (3/4/6 oz) and by the case (4 kg chicken cases at $85-100, 25-pack burgers at $35.50). Icon's bulk items have no case tiers.",
          "Owned weekly GTA delivery routes ($20-35, $100 minimum) plus free pickup. Food arrives frozen and on schedule, the exact point where Icon's 2026 reviews complain of thawed, weeks-late dry-ice shipments.",
          "Reputation: Google 4.8★ from 611 reviews, against Icon's Trustpilot 1.7 with unanswered 1-star reviews throughout 2026.",
          "A full butcher range for one-stop B2B supply: raw AAA, Prime and Wagyu beef, Ontario lamb, quarter cow, fish and Mediterranean grocery, alongside cooked meals.",
          "A chance to run a B2B funnel that works. Icon's Apply buttons go nowhere, so a J&W portal with an application, tiered pricing, net terms and standing route orders would beat the global reference."
        ],
        "correctedClaims": [
          "The selection says Icon has 'a separate wholesale login'. myaccount.iconmeals.com is Shopify's standard customer-account login (it redirects to a Shopify customer-account-api OAuth flow), not a bespoke portal. All three 'Apply Now' buttons on /pages/wholesale link to https://iconmeals.com (the homepage): there is no application form and no published wholesale pricing.",
          "The '8-meal minimum' is a site-wide shipping minimum for every customer, waived for pickup and 'select local partners'. It is not a wholesale-program term.",
          "'100% USDA-certified facility' is a company claim on the wholesale page. The how-it-works page says 'HACCP-certified kitchen' that passes state inspections daily.",
          "The partner logos (UFC, U.S. Olympic Team, Dallas Cowboys, USN, USA Triathlon, Absolute Recomp) are company claims. Only the 2021 UFC deal is independently sourced (ufc.com), and its 2026 status was not verified.",
          "competitors-global.md's '24-meal plan about $11' is out of date. The site now sells only 12- and 18-meal plans ($138-169 and $205-249), with a 5% Subscribe & Save discount (0% on Build Your Own).",
          "Garage Gym Reviews' 'nearly 1,000 Trustpilot reviews at 4.5 stars' is out of date. Trustpilot now shows a TrustScore of 1.7/5 on 980 reviews, and every review from May to Oct 2026 is 1-star.",
          "The founding year conflicts: 2014 per UFC.com and LinkedIn, 2012 per Garage Gym Reviews.",
          "Calling Icon 'premium' holds for price and brand partnerships but not for current service quality: 2026 reviews describe backlogs, thawed deliveries and unreachable support, and its blog stopped in Feb 2026."
        ],
