      {
        "name": "truLOCAL (https://trulocal.ca), owned by EMERGE Commerce, TSXV: ECOM",
        "verifiedOperating": true,
        "verificationNote": "Operating as of Oct 16, 2026. Evidence: (1) the Anti-Tariff Giveaway is live, running Oct 5 to Oct 31, 2026, with a draw around Nov 5, 2026 (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/); (2) the site footer reads © 2026 and the 10-year anniversary pages are live, including a \"Start Fresh in 2026\" free-meat offer (https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/); (3) Trustpilot shows reviews dated Oct 6 and Oct 8, 2026; (4) parent EMERGE posted Q2 2026 results on Aug 27, 2026, a refinancing on Sep 23, 2026 and a debenture notice on Oct 8, 2026, and its Jan 21, 2026 release names truLOCAL its largest brand (more than half of 2025 YTD revenue). It is premium in the mainstream sense: named-farm, 100% Canadian land proteins with grass-fed and organic lines. It is not all grass-fed or organic, though; the catalog also lists AAA and AA+ beef and RWA chicken, and there is no Wagyu. The pick holds, with corrections. Two caveats about this check: the web-search budget ran out mid-task, so I did not review social posts; and CBC returned HTTP 403, so the deal value was confirmed from EMERGE's own release instead. Notes are at /Users/erwingarcia/Claude/JWFOODSPROPOSAL/research/competitor-03.md. A key finding for the play: J&W's proposed tiers barely undercut truLOCAL per serving. Starter at $129 for ~30 servings is $4.30, against truLOCAL's $4.31. With J&W's $20 route fee added, J&W is at or above truLOCAL at every tier. The fix is to include free member delivery and size tiers to 36/80/104 servings, or to sell on $/lb. truLOCAL's own unit economics (CLTV of about $2,000 at an AOV of about $240) imply roughly 8 lifetime orders per customer, so the revenue lever must model churn rather than 11-12 boxes per subscriber per year.",
        "premiumEvidence": "The proteins are all Canadian-born, raised and processed land animals, from 100+ named partner farms. Examples are West Grey Premium Beef (Mount Forest), Heritage Cattle Co. (Keene, 100% grass-fed and finished), 3Gen Organics (certified organic pork), Yorkshire Valley Farms (organic chicken), Beverly Creek lamb and Fenwood Farms chicken (Ancaster), plus wild sockeye, lobster and scallops (https://trulocal.ca/our-suppliers?region=ON, https://trulocal.ca/faq). Product names include Grass-Fed and Finished Ribeye, Filet Mignon and Striploin, plus organic chicken; packs are flash-frozen and vacuum-sealed (https://trulocal.ca/products). Prices sit at a premium: $155-$349 subscription boxes, $170-$305 one-time boxes, and an AOV of about $240 in 2025 YTD (EMERGE release, Jan 21, 2026). Press logos shown are Forbes, Cision and Yahoo. It was on Dragons' Den in 2017 and ranked #14 in the Globe and Mail's Top Growing Companies 2020 (https://trulocal.ca/10-year). Limits on the premium claim: the catalog also carries AAA and AA+ beef and RWA chicken, and there is no Wagyu or Prime. Trustpilot is only 3.2/5 from 68 reviews (34% 1-star), with complaints about thawed boxes and price per lb (https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/trulocal.ca).",
        "pricing": "SUBSCRIPTION (https://trulocal.ca/pricing/, https://trulocal.ca/faq). Starter $155, up to 36 servings, 9 points (≈$4.31/serving, my maths). Family $279, up to 80 servings, 20 points (≈$3.49/serving). Mega $349, up to 104 servings, 26 points (≈$3.36/serving). Members can buy Extra Points to go over the limit (price not published). Shipping is free with no minimum; boxes ship in 1-3 days and arrive in 2-3. Members can skip, pause or cancel online with no contract, and a reminder email goes out 48 h before billing. Item prices are hidden; product pages show no price or weight. Referral: the new customer gets 15% off the first box and the referrer gets 500 Rewards Points.\n\nFREE MEAT FOR LIFE (https://trulocal.ca/free-life/, https://trulocal.ca/free-10yr/). New customers pick one item: 2 top sirloin steaks, 4 chicken breasts, 2 salmon fillets, or ground beef (2 packs on /free-life/, 1 pack on /free-10yr/). It comes in every box while the subscription is active, cannot be combined with other offers, and the 10-year version caps at 120 boxes.\n\nCURATED BOXES, one-time with free delivery and a $25 promo card (https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/). Backyard BBQ $170; Classic Clean & Lean $185; Chicken Lovers $185; Grass-Fed Steak Lovers $200; Essentials $210; Ultra Clean & Lean $250; Meat Me in the Grass $250.\n\nVALUE BOXES, one-time, sale price / struck-through price (https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/). Everyday Essentials $200/$251; Chicken Breast & Ground Beef $200/$252; Student $235/$279; AAA Striploin $235/$279; Chicken Breast $249/$279; Lean Ground Beef $249/$349; Classic Beef $270/$325; AAA Top Sirloin $279/$349; Anti-Tariff $279/$349; Grass-Fed Ribeye $299/$349; Grass-Fed Steak $305/$375. The Anti-Tariff box holds 14 chicken breasts, 7 lb lean ground beef, 6 lb lean ground chicken and 6 top sirloin steaks (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/). ESTIMATE: about 20.5-23 lb, or ≈$12-14/lb at $279 and ≈$15-17/lb at $349.\n\nJ&W COMPARISON (jw-catalog.json). AAA ribeye 10×11-12oz $175 = $23.33-25.45/lb. AAA striploin 8×8-9oz $110 = $24.44-27.50/lb. Portioned chicken breast case of about 4 kg $85 ≈ $9.64/lb. ESTIMATE: J&W could assemble an equivalent Anti-Tariff box for about $225 at current retail, though it is not like-for-like (AAA vs grass-fed and organic).",
        "scale": "Cumulative since 2016: 70,100+ customers, 511,000+ boxes, 100+ farm and producer partners, 14M+ meals, and 400+ active products as of Nov 2025 (https://trulocal.ca/10-year). Revenue was about $19.8M in 2020 (+130% YoY) at the time of acquisition. EMERGE bought it on Jan 4, 2021 for up to $16.8M: $6.5M cash plus 4,666,667 shares upfront, $1.5M deferred, and up to $4.5M earn-out. The founder/CEO was Marc Lafleur; HQ was Kitchener, with Vancouver, Calgary and Chicago facilities listed then (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2021/EMERGE-Acquires-truLOCAL-the-Market-Leader-in-Direct-to-Consumer-Meat-Subscriptions-for-up-to-16.8M). The Jan 21, 2026 EMERGE update gives: more than half of EMERGE's 2025 YTD revenue; AOV approaching $240 (2025 YTD) vs about $228 (2024); CLTV approaching $2,000; CAC about $100-175; two consecutive years of organic growth; Adjusted EBITDA roughly doubling in 2025; and EMERGE seeking tuck-in acquisitions of competitors and corporate-gifting businesses (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Provides-Corporate-Update-on-truLOCAL-as-the-Brand-Celebrates-its-10th-Anniversary). Net new subscriptions rose 193% YoY in Feb 2025 on the buy-Canadian surge (EMERGE, Mar 5, 2025). Parent figures across all brands: Q2 2026 revenue $9.11M and H1 2026 $15.02M (https://www.emerge-commerce.com/news/News/news-details/2026/EMERGE-Reports-Strong-Q2-2026-Results). Q1 2026 margin was hit by higher meat costs at truLOCAL (EMERGE MD&A). ESTIMATE: truLOCAL revenue is about $13M+/yr, below its 2020 peak, based on >50% of EMERGE revenue (EMERGE H1 2025 was $13.5M); low confidence. Trustpilot: 3.2/5 from 68 reviews.",
        "siteDesign": "Built as a custom web app (not Shopify) with images served through imgix. The page is light with lots of whitespace and alternates white bands with very pale green bands.\n\nPALETTE (from the live CSS at https://trulocal.ca/static/css/style.beebcab62128.css):\n- Primary CTA green #44A847, darker hover green #3D9740\n- Body text charcoal #353535, near-black #1E1E1E\n- Tinted section background #F6F9F6, hairline borders #DFE5DF\n- Sage accents #97CA98, #8C988C, #AFBCAF\n- Alert red #DD5151, orange #EC9744, info blue #56AEE5\n- The physical box is maple-leaf red on dark green/black with a yellow sticky-note label\n\nTYPOGRAPHY: Montserrat 300-800 throughout, letter-spacing -0.02em, heavy 700-800 headings. Nunito 700 and Lora are secondary; D-DIN Condensed is used for small uppercase badges. Buttons are pills (radius 3rem), weight 800 and about 56 px tall; cards have a 0.6rem radius.\n\nHOMEPAGE ORDER:\n1. Region selector (ON/AB/BC/Rest of Canada) and nav: Get Started / Pricing / Our Products / Curated Boxes / Giveaway / Corporate Gifts\n2. Offer hero with a \"Claim Offer\" pill and a free-shipping note\n3. Four value pillars\n4. Three-step How It Works\n5. Menu carousel of product cards (name and portion, no prices)\n6. A three-column comparison (truLOCAL vs grocery vs other meat boxes) with round check/x badges and tap-to-explain sheets\n7. Three testimonial cards with customer box photos\n8. Satisfaction guarantee\n9. Farm-partner cards\n10. Closing CTA, newsletter and press logos\n11. A floating \"Order Now / Free Shipping\" button\n\nPHOTOGRAPHY: overhead flat-lay on dark rustic wood, with white plates of grilled proteins around the branded box. Lifestyle shots show phones and hands. Testimonials use authentic customer unboxing photos of vacuum packs.\n\nTONE: warm, patriotic and community-led (\"buy Canadian\", \"I love local\", anti-tariff). Copy is plain and offer-heavy (free meat for life, free shipping, giveaways) with reassurance (pause anytime, no contracts).\n\nFOR J&W: keep the structure but use its own identity, for example oxblood/butcher red with cream and charcoal and a heritage serif or condensed pairing. Replace farm cards with \"inside our Ontario-inspected plant\" and three-generation heritage cards, and use a flat-lay mixing cooked Meal Butler portions with raw AAA and Wagyu steaks.",
        "keyFlows": [
          "SUBSCRIPTION SIGN-UP, 4 steps (https://trulocal.ca/get-started/). (1) Email + postal code to 'see if we serve your area', which captures the lead before any price is shown and comes with a consent line. (2) Choose box size: Starter/Family/Mega, sized by servings and points (9/20/26). (3) 'Pick your Proteins' points builder: each product costs points, Extra Points can be bought to go over, and items can roll over to the next order. (4) Checkout. The free-for-life item is pre-applied by promo-coded deep links (LIFECHK/LIFEBEEF/LIFESTK/LIFESLM).",
          "SUBSCRIPTION MANAGEMENT (https://trulocal.ca/faq). My Account > Manage Subscription > skip one box / pause for any length / change frequency / cancel, all online with no phone call or contract. A reminder email goes out 48 h before renewal so members can edit the box, and contents lock at billing.",
          "CORPORATE GIFTING, 4 steps (https://trulocal.ca/gifts/). (1) Choose boxes: curated, value, build-your-own, or gift cards of any amount. (2) Add a branded message. (3) Upload the recipient list. (4) truLOCAL packs and ships to each door, from the next business day or on a scheduled date. The quote form asks for quantity in bands (<10, 11-25, 26-50, 51-100, 100+). There is no minimum; volume discounts are mentioned but not published. A 24-hour response is promised by sales email or 1-800 number, and dietary or vegetarian swaps are handled on request.",
          "ONE-TIME BOX TO SUBSCRIBER CONVERSION (https://trulocal.ca/curated-boxes/, https://trulocal.ca/value-boxes/). A grid of no-subscription boxes with 'Buy Now' and free delivery. Value boxes show a struck-through regular price as an anchor. Curated boxes include a complimentary '$25 Promo Card', presumably to bring buyers back for a next order or subscription.",
          "REFERRAL (https://trulocal.ca/faq). The friend gets 15% off the first box (base price); the referrer gets 500 Rewards Points, redeemable for items in the next box; there is no cap on referrals.",
          "VIRAL CONTEST AS LEAD GEN (https://trulocal.ca/anti-tariff-giveaway/, Oct 5-31 2026). Entry is a form plus a skill-testing question. Bonus entries: +1 for sharing a referral link, +5 for each friend who enters through it, +20 for a purchase, +20 for a 100-word essay, which also produces user-generated content. Five prizes worth $279 each. The parent company owns the referral software Viral Loops.",
          "COMPARISON TABLE (https://trulocal.ca/). Three columns (truLOCAL / Major Grocery / Other Meat Boxes) against six rows: Canadian-sourced, fast fulfilment, free shipping, customisable, easy pause/skip/cancel, flash-frozen vacuum-sealed. The truLOCAL column is visually emphasised (4px border), and tapping it opens an explanation sheet.",
          "ANNIVERSARY / HERITAGE STORY PAGE (https://trulocal.ca/10-year). A dated timeline: first Instagram post, first box, Dragons' Den, 50K boxes, Globe ranking, acquisition, 500K boxes. Big cumulative counters (customers, boxes, farms, meals) sit next to an anniversary offer CTA."
        ],
        "featuresToBorrow": [
          "A points-based 'build your box' with tiers sized by servings. For J&W, show $/lb on every item, because truLOCAL hides item prices and that is a weakness to exploit.",
          "A 'free item in every box for life' hook with promo-coded deep links per item choice. For J&W, make the free item a Meal Butler cooked chicken pack so raw buyers try the cooked line. Cap it at a fixed number of boxes, as truLOCAL caps at 120.",
          "A postal-code + email gate as step one, so every visitor who starts becomes a lead even if they abandon. It would also check J&W route eligibility and show the delivery day (Tue/Wed/Thu/Fri route).",
          "Self-serve skip / pause / cancel plus a 48-hour pre-renewal email with an 'edit your box' link. This is low-churn hygiene that J&W lacks entirely.",
          "One-time 'value boxes' with a struck-through anchor price, plus a $25 next-order card in every one-time box to convert buyers into subscribers.",
          "A corporate-gift funnel: choose box > branded note > upload recipient CSV > J&W delivers on its routes. Add a quantity-band quote form and a 24-hour response SLA, timed for Q4 2026 holiday gifting. Note that provincially inspected meat must stay within Ontario.",
          "Give-15%/get-points referral, plus a seasonal viral contest with referral-weighted entries (e.g. 'Win a year of Meal Butler'), using J&W's existing Smile.io loyalty points.",
          "A three-column comparison table (J&W vs grocery vs other meat boxes) with tap-to-explain rows. J&W can add $/lb, custom cutting, cooked options and inspected-plant rows that truLOCAL cannot claim.",
          "A heritage timeline page with big counters (1973 Advance Food Products > 1995 J&W > 2008 Ontario-inspected plant > Meal Butler). J&W's story is three generations deep against truLOCAL's 10 years.",
          "Customer unboxing photos as testimonials and partner/plant story cards, alongside the 611 Google reviews J&W already has (4.8★).",
          "Free delivery included for subscribers. truLOCAL's free shipping with no minimum is a core promise, and J&W's $100 minimum + $20-35 fee would otherwise erase its price edge per serving."
        ],
        "whatJWDoesBetter": [
          "It owns an Ontario-inspected meat plant (since 2008) and cuts, portions, cooks and vacuum-seals in-house. truLOCAL aggregates 100+ suppliers and names no own plant or inspection body (https://trulocal.ca/about-us/).",
          "Heritage: a family butcher and wholesale lineage from 1973 (Advance Food Products) and J&W from 1995, vs truLOCAL's 2016 start.",
          "A cooked, weighed, portioned, à-la-carte Meal Butler line with no added salt, sugar or oil (e.g. 4oz cooked chicken breast $4.20-$4.99). truLOCAL offers no à-la-carte items (https://trulocal.ca/faq) and only a few prepared SKUs (pizza, pierogi, pot pies, BBQ chicken).",
          "Price per lb (J&W catalog): AAA ribeye 10×11-12oz $175 = $23-25/lb; portioned chicken breast case of about 4 kg $85 ≈ $9.64/lb; Wagyu/Kobe AAA ground $14.99/lb. ESTIMATE: J&W could assemble truLOCAL's $279-sale/$349-regular Anti-Tariff box for about $225, though it is not like-for-like (AAA vs grass-fed).",
          "A deeper premium range than truLOCAL: Wagyu striploin 8-9oz $35, Canadian Prime ribeye, bison, venison, a whole Ontario lamb $400, a quarter cow $1,200, and whole striploin/ribeye cut to any thickness. truLOCAL sells fixed portions only and lists no Wagyu.",
          "A shorter, controlled GTA cold chain: scheduled routes from Scarborough, free store pickup, and 1-2 day express in core GTA. truLOCAL uses couriers with a 24-36 h frozen window, and thawed-box complaints recur in its Trustpilot reviews.",
          "Stronger social proof: J&W has Google 4.8★ from 611 reviews vs truLOCAL's Trustpilot 3.2/5 from 68 reviews (different platforms, so treat as directional).",
          "Bulk and case buying for athletes, families and food-service (4 kg cases, 55 lb chicken), plus a physical butcher counter and local relationships."
        ],
