      {
        "name": "WeCook (WeCook for Business), Montreal, QC",
        "verifiedOperating": true,
        "verificationNote": "WeCook was operating and expanding on October 16, 2026.\n- Its own API publishes weekly menus through Nov 8-14, 2026. Menu #422 covers the week of Oct 18, 2026, with 15 meals, and /en/week-menu renders \"Week of October 18 2026\".\n- The footer reads © 2026, and the server's Date header was Oct 16, 2026.\n- Trustpilot shows 4.8 from 6,584 reviews, 672 of them in the last 12 months. The newest reviews are dated Oct 6-8, 2026.\n- The About page and FAQ now say \"Quebec, Ontario, the Maritimes, and now Western Canada\". The tax config includes BC, AB, SK and MB. A retail \"fresh aisle\" line is live.\n- The B2B landing page (/en/business) and the full employer-dashboard copy are live.\n\nLimitation: the shared web-search budget was used up, so I ran no 2026 news search for funding, ownership or layoffs. Status rests on WeCook's own live data, Trustpilot and 2025 trade press (Retail Insider and Strategy, both re-fetched today). Notes are written to /Users/erwingarcia/Claude/JWFOODSPROPOSAL/research/competitor-04.md.",
        "premiumEvidence": "WeCook is premium mass-market, not luxury.\n- The executive chef is Gabriel Drapeau. Retail Insider describes the menu as backed by \"an executive chef from Montreal's celebrated restaurant Joe Beef\"; WeCook's own site names Drapeau but does not mention Joe Beef.\n- Meals are fresh, never frozen and preservative-free. The menu has 15 new meals a week from 500+ recipes, with 4 premium surcharge dishes each week.\n- Brand collaborations: two with P.K. Subban (the second ran Sep 7-Oct 4, 2025, per Strategy) and a Schwartz's Deli pop-up in Toronto on Jul 30, 2025 at Avenue Diner, 222 Davenport Rd, with $5 dishes. A collaboration with Maripier Morin was announced next.\n- Trustpilot: 4.8 from 6,584 reviews (81% 5-star).\n- Scale: 90,000 meals a week and about 400 staff (Retail Insider, Aug 26, 2025); \"+4 million meals/yr\", \"+150,000 unique customers\", demand \"+1000% since 2020\" and two production facilities (About page).\n- Ownership: Desjardins Capital, Investissement Québec and Claridge Food Group as majority holder.\n- Revenue was not found. ESTIMATE: 90K meals/wk × ~$13 × 52 ≈ $60M/yr gross.",
        "pricing": "Live from /api/plans on Oct 16, 2026, in CAD per meal. WeCook says its meals are not taxable.\n\n| Plan (meals/week) | Small (300-400 g) | Regular (440-550 g) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6 | $14.49 | $15.49 |\n| 9 | $13.49 | $14.49 |\n| 14 | $12.99 | $13.99 |\n| 22-30 | $11.99 | $12.99 |\n\n- Family size (serves 4-5) is $29.95 and counts toward the plan.\n- Premium surcharges for the week of Oct 18: +$1.95 or +$4.25.\n- Minimum order is 6 meals.\n- Delivery: the FAQ says $7.99 (first delivery free with a promo), but two other FAQ entries say delivery is free.\n- Homepage offer: $100 off across the first 4 orders (PROMO100).\n- Add-ons: 4 egg bites $9.99 (was $11.99), 6 protein bites $13.99, 4 protein brownies or cookies $14.95, 4×300 mL juices $20.95, Mox shake $13.99.\n- Gift cards: $25, $50, $100, $200 or custom ($10 minimum), with a live +10% bonus.\n- Loyalty: a $20 credit costs 50,000 WeCoins.\n\nWeCook for Business (CEO, Retail Insider, Aug 2025):\n- \"$11.75-ish\" per meal on the platform, against $20-25 for catering.\n- The employer contributes from \"$1 per meal, or 100%\".\n- Fits companies of 20-500 employees. Larger firms get on-site fridges.\n- The dashboard says meal prices adjust to the total volume ordered by all employees.",
        "siteDesign": "Layout:\n- Sticky white header: a lowercase green wordmark (B2B adds a \"for business\" sub-label), an FR/EN toggle, a solid green CTA and a hamburger menu.\n- Consumer hero: split. A white rounded card on the left holds the H1, the subhead, a pale-yellow promo pill, an email field with CTA, and a \"Proudly Canadian\" stamp. A full-bleed photo of green branded boxes and clear trays on marble fills the right.\n- B2B hero: 50/50. A lifestyle photo of office workers eating from trays sits on the left. On the right are a Trustpilot strip, a big green H1 and the lead form, which works as the hero itself.\n- B2B section order: an auto-scrolling benefit marquee; 3 numbered \"Set it up once\" step cards; an asymmetric \"Perks\" photo pair; a 2×2 \"Myth-Busters\" photo grid that handles objections; a cream logo band; a repeat form block; a 5-column footer.\n- Menu page: round-icon category tabs, a week picker, and a 4-column card grid. Cards carry square photos, a green circular protein sticker (\"52 g protein\"), a \"Family size available\" outline pill and a dark-green \"Premium meals\" band.\n\nPalette (from CSS):\n\n| Role | Hex |\n|---|---|\n| Primary green | #03894E / #00894E |\n| Darker greens | #026B3D, #027040, #025732 |\n| Text charcoal | #232522 (headings grey #393C3F) |\n| Mid grey | #979797 |\n| Cream bands | #FBF8EA, #F9F4EF, #FBFAF6 |\n| Promo yellow | #FDCF32 / #FAB418 |\n| Family-size orange | #FF9D1E / #F37601 |\n| Error red | #F44336 |\n| Base | white |\n\nTypography:\n- Headings: Rubik 700 in green. H1 48 px, H2 32 px, H3 24 px.\n- Body: museo-sans at 16/24.\n- Accents: Fierce Vector, a heavy condensed display face, on the retail page, and Permanent Marker as a handwritten accent.\n- Buttons: 8 px radius, white on green, 14-16 px bold. Inputs: 44 px tall, 8 px radius.\n\nPhotography: bright natural light. Food is shot top-down on linen, stone or marble in clear trays or ceramic bowls. Lifestyle images show diverse people eating at work or at a family table. The retail line uses colour-blocked pink and mustard studio backgrounds with close-ups of someone eating.\n\nTone: friendly, punchy and benefit-first, with light humour (\"Heat, eat, repeat\", \"Culture is built in moments, not meetings\", \"Your office fridge says more about your company than your About Us page\"). It is bilingual. B2B copy speaks about HR outcomes: productivity ROI, \"cheaper than churn\".",
        "keyFlows": [
          "B2B signup and employer onboarding:\n1. /en/business shows a hero lead form above the fold. Fields: first name, last name, email, company name, phone; a 'For who' dropdown (Business / Gym / Sports team / Retail / Other / For me, where For me redirects to consumer plans); number of employees; notes (200 characters maximum); and an 'I am already a WeCook client' checkbox.\n2. The 'Talk to our team' button leads to a success message, 'Your business account request has been delivered'. Sales follows up within business days.\n3. Once approved, the admin receives an email with a temporary password for the business portal.\n4. A guided tour runs: Business Program, then invite employees, then manage invitations.",
          "B2B allowance engine:\n- One default allowance applies to all employees, with per-employee overrides.\n- Allowance types: $X per employee on all items; $X per employee on meals only; N meals per employee; or $X per meal up to a cap.\n- Caps: max meals per employee, max meals paid by the company, and max per order.\n- Meal price is volume-tiered across the whole company's weekly order.",
          "B2B employee management and weekly cycle:\n1. The admin invites employees by email. The address must match the company's email domain.\n2. The admin sees Pending and Accepted lists, can resend an invite after 24h, and can delete a user, which removes that user's future selections.\n3. Each employee picks meals weekly. Personal and corporate accounts are kept separate, and a migration flow handles existing consumers.\n4. The admin sees the joint order with a meal overview and a live 'Refresh order' button.\n5. A week with no orders auto-cancels.\n6. Delivery goes to the office, 7am-5pm.\n7. The receipt and invoice split 'Total covered by the company' from 'Total paid by the employee(s)'.\n8. Business accounts cannot use the app yet.",
          "Consumer subscription:\n1. Email capture in the hero, tied to the $100-off offer.\n2. Choose a delivery date and location: postal code, home or office.\n3. Choose a size (Small or Regular), then a plan (6, 9, 14 or 22-30 meals).\n4. Split the count between individual and family-size meals.\n5. Pick from 15 meals and customise the side (rice & veg, potatoes & veg, quinoa & veg, or veg only).\n6. Set preferences: ingredients to exclude and a preferred side style.\n7. Enter delivery details and pay by Visa, MC, Amex, Apple Pay or Google Pay, with an email OTP.",
          "Subscription management:\n- Plan up to 5 weeks ahead and skip weeks.\n- Weeks you don't fill are auto-selected from your preferences. The opt-in 'Premium Feature' lets auto-selection include surcharge meals and earns WeCoins.\n- Add snacks or oats on a recurring weekly or biweekly frequency.\n- Cancelling triggers a retention flow, and accumulated WeCoins are forfeited.",
          "Gift card:\n1. Choose an amount ($25, $50, $100, $200, or custom with a $10 minimum). A live +10% bonus is shown.\n2. Personalise: recipient name and email, sender, and a message.\n3. Pick the date the email is sent, or 'Print yourself'.\n4. Optionally send the bonus card to a different person.\n5. Pay by card or with WeCoins.\n6. The recipient activates the card at /activate-gift-cards.",
          "Gamified loyalty and referral:\n- Members earn WeCoins on orders, on badges (first breakfast order, first sous-vide order) and on social challenges (FB, IG, TikTok).\n- Levels: Bronze 0-29,999, Silver 30,000-59,999, Gold 60,000+.\n- The WeCoins Market redeems coins for credit ($20 = 50,000 coins), freebies such as egg bites or a shaker, or gift cards.\n- Referral reward: 'Refer 6 friends = 1 year of FREE snacks'."
        ],
        "featuresToBorrow": [
          "An employer allowance engine with four subsidy types (per employee on all items, per employee on meals only, N meals, or per meal up to a cap) and three caps (meals per employee, meals paid by the company, max per order), with per-employee overrides",
          "Employee invites locked to the company's email domain, with Pending and Accepted lists, a resend after 24h, and deletion that removes the person's future picks",
          "One joint weekly order per site that auto-cancels if nobody orders, with a live 'Refresh order' roll-up and a meal overview for the office manager",
          "An invoice and receipt that split the company-paid share from the employee-paid share, emailed after each order",
          "Company-wide volume pricing: the meal price drops as total site volume rises, which gives HR a reason to push participation",
          "A B2B lead form in the hero with a 'For who' segment (Business / Gym / Sports team / Retail / Other). One page routes leads to both J&W at Work and the Pro Partner Portal",
          "A 'Myth-Busters' section that answers HR objections: cost-sharing, lost lunch hours, no cafeteria, the freezer as a perk",
          "A tiered plan ladder (6/9/14/22 meals) with a falling per-meal price, plus a family-size option that counts toward the plan",
          "Planning up to 5 weeks ahead, skip-a-week, and auto-selection from saved preferences, with an opt-in to include premium surcharge dishes",
          "Protein-gram stickers on menu card photos, and full nutrition facts per portion size",
          "Gift cards with a seasonal +10% bonus, a scheduled send date, a print-yourself option, sending the bonus card to someone else, and paying with points",
          "Gamified loyalty (coins, badges, levels and a rewards market) plus a referral ladder ('refer 6 = 1 year free snacks')",
          "Limited-time celebrity and local-institution collaborations (P.K. Subban, the Schwartz's pop-up). For J&W, this could be a Toronto athlete or a Danforth Greek institution, with a 'chef/butcher' video series"
        ],
        "whatJWDoesBetter": [
          "A real butcher with an Ontario-inspected plant (since 2008) and family meat heritage back to the 1960s, Advance Food Products (1973) and J&W (1995). WeCook is a ready-meal kitchen: its raw-meat landing and DTC vacuum-sealed line appear wound down (the site string reads 'We no longer offer vacuum-sealed foods'). J&W can sell an office both the lunches and the steaks or gift boxes",
          "À la carte, macro-exact cooked portions: chicken breast 4 oz for $4.20-4.99, salmon 6 oz for $11.00, pre-set meals $8.99-14.99 in 4 or 6 oz protein, with no plan minimum. WeCook sells fixed dishes in two sizes with a 6-meal minimum",
          "Salt-free, sugar-free, no added oil. WeCook's Protein Power Bowl has 795-1,015 mg sodium and contains canola oil and salt. That is a strong pitch to hospitals, clinics and wellness-driven HR teams",
          "A frozen, vacuum-sealed format suits an unattended office freezer with no weekly use-by pressure and less waste. WeCook's fresh trays must be eaten by the expiry date, and it has to supply fridges",
          "Price parity or better: J&W pre-set meals at $9.99-12.99 sit at or below WeCook's $11.99-15.49 plan prices. Egg bites are about equal per piece (J&W ~$2.13-2.75 vs WeCook ~$2.50)",
          "GTA home turf: a Scarborough plant and store with free pickup, and its own weekly routes (GTA 2 on Wednesday covers Toronto, Scarborough and North York; GTA 4 on Friday covers Markham, Vaughan and Richmond Hill). WeCook ships from Quebec through third-party couriers, and its B2B logo wall is Quebec firms",
          "Breadth for one-stop B2B and household orders: raw cuts, Wagyu, whole and half animals, Greek specialties, supplements and snacks"
        ],
