        "scale": "- Volume and staff (Retail Insider, Aug 26, 2025, so 14 months old): about 90,000 meals a week and nearly 400 employees.\n- Company claims (About page, Oct 2026): \"+4 million meals delivered per year\", \"+150,000 unique customers\", demand \"+1000% since 2020\", \"600 new jobs\" and \"two production facilities\".\n- History and ownership: founded in 2012 as Nutrition Fit Plus. Owners are Desjardins Capital, Investissement Québec and Claridge Food Group as majority holder. The CEO is Michel Gagné.\n- Reviews: Trustpilot 4.8 from 6,584 reviews (672 in the last 12 months).\n- Markets: QC, ON, the Maritimes, and now Western Canada.\n- Channels: iOS and Android apps, plus a retail fresh-aisle line.\n- Revenue: not found. ESTIMATE: about $60M/yr gross (90K × ~$13 × 52).",
        "correctedClaims": [
          "The selection says Retail Insider was 'verified Oct 16 2026'. The page was re-fetched today, but the article is dated Aug 26, 2025. The 90K meals/week, ~400 staff, $11.75/meal, $1-100% subsidy and 20-500 employee figures are 14 months old. The B2B page and dashboard copy are still live, so the program itself is current",
          "Scope is out of date. The selection says WeCook delivers to 'Ontario incl. GTA, Quebec, New Brunswick and the Halifax area'. WeCook now says 'Quebec, Ontario, the Maritimes, and now Western Canada' (FAQ and About, Oct 2026)",
          "The Joe Beef link rests only on Retail Insider's wording. WeCook's own site names its executive chef, Gabriel Drapeau, but never mentions Joe Beef",
          "The Strategy PK Subban article covers the second collaboration (P.K.'s Spaghetti and Meatballs, Sep 7-Oct 4, 2025), not a standing partnership",
          "An earlier note said the consumer price list was 'not found, $10-13 anecdotal'. The live API now shows $11.99-15.49 per meal on plans, with Family size at $29.95",
          "'Fresh, never frozen' holds at delivery, but the FAQ says customers can freeze meals, so the freezer argument should not claim WeCook meals cannot be frozen",
          "Delivery cost is unclear. The FAQ contradicts itself: one answer says $7.99 (first delivery free with a promo), while two others say delivery is free"
        ]
      },
      {
        "name": "Omaha Steaks (Business Gifts)",
        "verifiedOperating": true,
        "verificationNote": "I checked on October 16, 2026, using a real browser because omahasteaks.com returns HTTP 403 (Akamai bot block) to WebFetch and curl. Every page loads normally in a browser.\n- Live: the footer reads \"© 2026 OmahaSteaks.com LLC … v26.10.7\", the nav shows Christmas Gifts and a Semi-Annual Sale (code SEMI25), and the business-gifts page is current.\n- Recent news: a Sept 9-10, 2026 press release announced a business transformation and the new \"No Bull\" brand. Average delivery fell from \"more than six days in early 2025 to two days today\". On Sept 22, 2026 it opened a Grapevine, TX store offering free same-day or next-day delivery within 75 miles.\n- Premium and heritage: founded 1917, run by the fifth-generation Simon family (Todd Simon is Chairman; Nate Rempe is President and CEO). Its press boilerplate calls it \"the nation's largest direct response marketer of premium beef and gourmet foods\".\n- Key caveat: \"Omaha Steaks does not ship to Canada\" (Shipping page). It is a model to learn from, not a GTA rival, and it leaves the GTA premium \"choice\" meat-gift space open for J&W.\n- Gap: web-search budget ran out mid-task, so revenue and third-party news were not found. All figures come from Omaha's own pages, the CRA, or J&W's own files.",
        "premiumEvidence": "- 109-year heritage brand (1917), \"America's Original Butcher\", fifth-generation family-owned. It runs two processing plants and two distribution centres in Omaha, NE, and has \"nearly 50\" US stores (44 in the store finder).\n- Beef aged 35+ days; \"900+ years combined\" master-butcher experience.\n- Business-gifting claims (\"Omaha Steaks internal data in 2025\"): 5,000+ business clients, 350k+ custom gift certificates and 700k+ coolers delivered to business recipients since 2020.\n- A commissioned Harris Poll (Apr 2023, n=2,004) found 89% say a gift increases happiness at work and 68% prefer quality food over branded merchandise.\n- Social proof: the Steakhouse Grill Gift has 4.5 stars from 686 reviews.\n- Business Rewards tiers run up to President's Club Elite at US$50,000+ a year in spend.\n- A 100% guarantee, a 24/7 phone line, dedicated Business Gift Experts, and an AI chat with voice capability (Simple AI).",
        "pricing": "All prices USD.\n\nBusiness gift boxes on the business-gifts page:\n- Celebration Grill Gift $81 (14 items)\n- Steakhouse Grill Gift $148 (16 items)\n- Tasteful Business Gift $211 (22 items)\n- Ribeye Reserve Collection $212 (22 items, ships free)\n\n2026 Holiday Gift Guide (\"From $100-$200\", all ship free):\n- Holiday Steak Dinner Gift $100\n- Holiday Entertainer Collection $150\n- Surf & Turf Celebration $175\n- Steak Lovers Holiday Collection $180\n\nChoice Gift Certificate levels are NOT published (\"Ask About Price Levels\"). The rewards page implies a Platinum certificate worth about $200 and a Diamond worth about $250.\n\nVolume discounts are NOT published (\"may be available for qualifying orders\"). The lead form's buckets are only for sorting leads:\n- Gift count: <10 / 10-50 / 50-100 / 100-200 / 200-500 / 500+\n- Spend per gift: <$100 / $100-150 / $150-250 / >$250\n\nPay-on-redemption is available for qualifying large programs. Payment is a single invoice or one card charge.\n\nBusiness Rewards tiers by annual spend:\n- Member (free)\n- Premier $5,000+: free logo placement and a $200 Platinum certificate\n- President's Club $20,000+: $400 in certificates, a $100 box and 10% personal savings\n- Elite $50,000+: $500 in Diamond certificates and ten $100 gift cards\n\nConsumer terms:\n- Shipping $24.99 under $149, free at $149+, plus $109 to Alaska/Hawaii\n- Subscribe and save 10%\n- $10 off $50+ in store\n- Current promo: $25 off $149+ (SEMI25)",
        "scale": "- Business gifting: 5,000+ business clients, 350k+ corporate gift certificates and 700k+ business-gift coolers since 2020 (company claim, 2025 internal data).\n- Retail: about 50 US stores.\n- Facilities: two plants and two distribution centres in Omaha, NE.\n- Delivery: 2-day average as of Sept 2026; same-day ship if ordered by 6pm ET.\n- Reviews: product pages carry hundreds of reviews (686 on the Steakhouse Grill Gift).\n- Revenue and funding: not found (private, family-owned; search budget used up).\n\nESTIMATE: (350k + 700k) / 5,000 clients ≈ 210 gifts per business client over about 5 years. That works out to roughly 40 gifts per client per year, which points to a mid-size-program business.",
        "siteDesign": "Layout:\n- Header: white, with a centred crest logo, \"Shop All\" and a horizontally scrolling category nav. A thin deep-burgundy promo strip sits beneath it.\n- Hero: full-bleed dark photo, left-aligned white headline and one white pill CTA (\"Get Volume Pricing\").\n- Sections: centred h2s between hairline rules. The proof band is four big numerals.\n- Program cards: three white tiles with line icons (Employee / Client / Marketing).\n- Feature rows: an image on the left, a stacked four-item benefit list on the right.\n- Lower page: a case study in Challenge / Solution / Result format, article cards, a shoppable product row with \"Customize\" and \"Add to Cart\", and a sticky action row (Call / Order Form / Quote / Gift Guide).\n- Business subdomain pages: an \"at a Glance\" summary card at top right of the hero, check-mark benefit rows, numbered step cards, tier cards (START HERE / PREMIER / ENHANCED / HIGHEST), an FAQ accordion and an inline lead form.\n- Pop-ups and chat: a lead-capture modal opens on arrival, and a right-edge \"Chat\" tab stays visible.\n\nPalette (from computed CSS):\n- Text: near-black #231F20 (#1F1F1F on the business subdomain), on white #FFFFFF.\n- Brand: deep burgundy #890419 (promo strip and primary pill buttons, radius 999px), logo red #CC2127, link burgundy #862732.\n- Greys: warm grey #CDC7C2, warm off-white #F5F3F1, cool greys #D1D3D4 / #E9E9E9 / #F6F6F6, mid grey #5D5D5D, cream #FAF7F2.\n- Rare accents: teal #00797F, chat blue #165077.\n\nTypography:\n- Main-site H1: neue-aachen-pro, a bold condensed slab-serif display (52px).\n- Main-site H2/H3/body: neue-haas-grotesk-display grotesk at 32 / 24 / 16px, weights 600 and 450.\n- Business subdomain: plain Arial Bold (H1 51px, -1.28px tracking), so the two sites look inconsistent.\n\nPhotography:\n- Moody, low-key close-ups: hands holding the branded red box, seared steaks in cast iron with herbs and steam, the box on a brick doorstep.\n- Warm tungsten tones, shallow depth of field.\n\nTone of voice:\n- Confident and service-led (\"Corporate Gifts Made Easy\", \"We help handle the rest\").\n- Quantified proof everywhere, heritage-driven, and now the self-aware \"No Bull\" voice.\n\nFor J&W:\n- Keep the dark hero, big-number proof band, at-a-Glance card, step cards and tier cards.\n- Swap in J&W's brand colour, with Mediterranean olive and terracotta as accents.\n- Swap the story for the Trougakos family story (1960s Toronto butcher shop, 1973, 1995, Ontario-inspected plant 2008).",
        "keyFlows": [
          "LEAD CAPTURE MODAL (opens when the business-gifts page loads):\n1) Headline 'Business Gift Offers' promising volume discounts, branded gifts and easy ordering.\n2) Fields: First, Last, Business Name, Work Email, Business Phone, Comments.\n3) Gift count picked with one tap of a pill button: <10 / 10-50 / 50-100 / 100-200 / 200-500 / 500+.\n4) Spend per gift picked the same way: <$100 / $100-150 / $150-250 / >$250.\n5) A Business Gift Expert calls back, and the lead arrives already qualified.",
          "CHOICE GIFT CERTIFICATE (6 steps):\n1) Choose the certificate level, quantity, timing and payment structure, including pay-on-redemption for qualifying large programs.\n2) Customize with logo, message, recipient name, campaign title or occasion wording. Logos only for qualifying programs.\n3) Distribute by mailing to each recipient, shipping in bulk to the office, or handing out at events. Formats: Printed-Bulk, Printed-Mailed or E-Certificate.\n4) The recipient redeems online, by phone or by mail with a unique code.\n5) The recipient picks from the curated collections at that level.\n6) The gift ships, with standard shipping included.\nUse cases listed: employee recognition, client appreciation, sales incentives/SPIFs, safety awards, loyalty and promotions, events and golf outings.",
          "MULTI-RECIPIENT ORDER (4 steps):\n1) Describe the program: audience, count, budget per recipient, occasion, timing.\n2) Pick catalogue gifts or have an expert curate a custom box.\n3) Fill in one Business Gift Order Form, an Excel .xlsx hosted on Pardot, with each recipient's name, address, gift and message.\n4) Email it to largeorders@ (or fax or phone). Card details are taken by phone; one dedicated expert coordinates shipments; pay with a single invoice or one card charge.\nWith no addresses, switch to Choice certificates. Repeat years: the client simply sends the account manager an updated list.",
          "CUSTOMIZE YOUR BOX (points builder):\n1) Start from a preset gift, e.g. Steakhouse Grill Gift at $148.\n2) Every item carries a point value (chicken breasts 1 pt, NY strips 2-6 pts, Private Reserve filets 12 pts); the box needs at least 5 points with no maximum.\n3) Browse category tabs: Steaks / Burgers & Franks / Chicken & Pork / Seafood / Meals, Sides & Sweets.\n4) A sticky footer shows 'Reset Box · X Points Used · $price · Continue →' under a two-step progress bar (Home, then Customize).",
          "BUSINESS REWARDS (annual spend tiers):\n1) Join free through the form, which asks gift count and gift timing (within 30 days / 1-3 months / 3-6 months / later / holiday season).\n2) Order through the year.\n3) Move up tiers as annual qualified spend grows: Premier $5K+, President's Club $20K+, Elite $50K+.\n4) Receive tier rewards: free logo placement, Platinum and Diamond certificates, 10% personal savings, and $100 gift cards that can be passed on to staff or clients.",
          "HOLIDAY PLANNING CALENDAR (content-led nurture):\n- July: set goals and budget.\n- August: review options (the August-October 'planning window').\n- September: select gifts and gather recipients.\n- October: finalise branding and quantities.\n- November: schedule early deliveries.\n- December: certificates cover late needs.\nPaired with a 'Plan Your Holiday Gift Program' CTA, a free Gift Guide download and a right-edge AI chat (Simple AI, voice capable)."
        ],
        "featuresToBorrow": [
          "J&W Choice e-certificate: one code per recipient, redeemable for a box, a Meal Butler week or a Butcher Club month. The recipient books delivery on their J&W route day or picks up at Brimley Rd, which solves the perishable-delivery and unknown-address problems. Under CRA rules (page modified 2026-07-27) it stays a non-cash gift under the $500/yr limit only if it can be used at a single retailer and cannot be turned into cash.",
          "Pay-on-redemption for programs of 50+ recipients: the buyer pays only for codes actually redeemed. It is a strong close, and unredeemed codes cost J&W nothing to produce.",
          "Two-tap lead qualifier (gift count, then spend per gift) on a business landing page, routed to an AI concierge or sales follow-up.",
          "A points-based 'Customize Your Box' builder with a sticky footer (points used, price, Continue). Use it for corporate tiers and for a weekly Meal Butler builder.",
          "Annual-spend Business Rewards tiers in CAD, e.g. free logo placement and a personal-use certificate at C$5K+, more at C$20K+. They lock in repeat annual programs.",
          "Recipient-list ordering with a single invoice. J&W can go further than Omaha's emailed Excel file with an in-browser CSV upload, live preview and route-day auto-assignment.",
          "A holiday and occasion planning calendar with 'Plan your program' CTAs: year-end, Greek Easter, client thank-yous, realtor closings, safety milestones, employee anniversaries.",
          "A proof band of big numbers and a Challenge/Solution/Result case study once J&W has its first 3-5 corporate clients. Add product ratings: Omaha shows 686 reviews on one gift, J&W shows none.",
          "Stores as fulfilment hubs, the way Omaha cut delivery from 6+ days to 2. J&W's Brimley Rd plant is already inside its delivery radius: 'GTA delivery on your route day, or pickup today.'",
          "Use-case tiles (employee recognition, client appreciation, sales SPIFs, safety awards, events and golf outings) to widen gifting beyond December."
        ],
        "whatJWDoesBetter": [
          "It actually serves the GTA. Omaha Steaks does not ship to Canada. J&W runs weekly routes across Toronto, Durham, York, Peel, Halton and Hamilton, plus long-haul to Ottawa, London, Windsor and Kingston, at a flat $20-$35 ($100 minimum), with free pickup in Scarborough. Omaha charges US$24.99 per address under $149.",
          "Ontario-inspected meat plant since 2008 in Scarborough, cut to order. Its chicken is air-chilled, grain-fed and Ontario-raised with no hormones or antibiotics, a local story Omaha cannot tell in Canada.",
          "Toronto butcher heritage: George Trougakos's downtown butcher-grocer from the early 1960s, Advance Food Products in 1973, J&W in 1995. It is a local family story of the same kind as Omaha's.",
          "Cooked, portioned meals à la carte: about 292 Meal Butler items, salt- and sugar-free with no added oils, in 4-8 oz portions. That makes a corporate 'wellness gift', such as a week of clean meals, possible. Omaha's prepared range is indulgent (scalloped potatoes, tartlets, Chicken Kiev, nuggets).",
          "Greek and Mediterranean identity: A Taste of Greece Box at $130 and about 154 Mediterranean SKUs, which suits GTA Greek-Canadian businesses and Greek Easter gifting.",
          "Comparable price points in CAD: Ultimate BBQ Dad Pack $84.99, Girls Night $89.99, Gentleman's Box $129.99, Taste of Greece $130, Long Weekend $199, against Omaha's US$81-$212 boxes. That is a meaningful saving once the exchange rate and per-address shipping are counted.",
          "Wholesale and whole-animal depth (AAA/Prime/Wagyu, cases, quarter cow $1,200, whole lamb) for a premium 'J&W Reserve' tier and office-catering add-ons."
        ],
        "correctedClaims": [
          "The pick said the business-gifts page 'returned 403 on re-check'. It is live. The 403 is Akamai bot protection blocking WebFetch and curl. The page loaded normally in a browser on Oct 16, 2026, and shows the 5,000+ / 350k+ / 700k+ figures labelled 'Omaha Steaks internal data in 2025'.",
          "The pick described 'volume pricing at bands from under 10 to 500+ recipients'. Those bands are quantity buckets on the lead and quote form. No volume-discount tiers or percentages are published; the site says only that discounts 'may be available for qualifying orders'.",
          "The pick said 'Choice certificates carry a company logo'. Logos are offered only 'for qualifying custom programs'. Free logo placement is a Business Rewards Premier benefit, which requires US$5,000+ in annual spend.",
          "Choice certificate price levels are not published (the page says 'Ask About Price Levels'). The rewards page implies a Platinum certificate at about US$200 and a Diamond at about US$250.",
          "Omaha's B2B flow is not self-serve. It runs on a lead form, an expert or account manager, and an emailed Excel order form. A self-serve CSV-upload studio would put J&W ahead of Omaha on ease of use.",
          "Omaha does not ship to Canada, so it is a benchmark for running a business-gifting program, not a GTA competitor for the same buyers.",
          "J&W-side issues to fix before launch: gift cards exist only as e-cards ($10/$25/$50/$100 CAD) with no corporate or branded option, and the 'A Taste of Greece Box Special' product description contains pasted voice-dictation text ('Do you know what I know who it is so yeah don't worry…'). Fix it before selling the box to companies."
        ],
