      {
        "name": "Halenda's Meats / Halenda's Fundraising (Oshawa, Durham Region)",
        "verifiedOperating": true,
        "verificationNote": "Operating in October 2026.\n- The weekly sale page says \"Sale runs from Thursday, October 15th – Wednesday, October 21st, 2026\".\n- A main-site post dated Oct 6, 2026 announces the 2026 Ontario's Finest Meat Diamond Award (Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon).\n- On the fundraising site, the Purchase, Product and About pages were updated Sept 23, 2026. New success-story posts went up Aug 24 and Sept 2, 2026. Both sites show ©2026.\n\nPremium only in part. Halenda's is an award-winning craft smokehouse:\n- 3 Diamond Awards (2017, 2024, 2026) and 5 Awards of Excellence.\n- An Iberico-style line, and charcuterie in Metro, Sobeys, Foodland and Fortinos.\n\nBut it positions itself on \"Quality, Service and Low Prices\": pork tenderloin $4.99/lb, schnitzel $3.99/lb. The fundraising arm sells one mass-market pork pepperoni stick. Use it as the fundraising and freezer-pack channel benchmark, not as a luxury benchmark.\n\nLimits:\n- The web-search budget was exhausted, so there is no third-party news, reviews or revenue data.\n- Instagram and Facebook were not checked (login walls).\n- All facts come from Halenda's own three domains, plus the J&W catalog for comparisons.\n\nNotes: /Users/erwingarcia/Claude/JWFOODSPROPOSAL/research/competitor-07.md",
        "premiumEvidence": "Quality signals:\n- 2026 Diamond Award, the single highest-scoring product of the biennial Ontario's Finest Meat Competition, for Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon. It is the third Diamond, after 2017 Double Smoked Bacon and 2024 Ruby Red Bacon.\n- Six products awarded in 2026:\n  - Platinum: Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon; Iberico Premium Koby Dog.\n  - Gold: Iberico BBQ Sausage; Dill Pickle Pepperoni Stick.\n  - Silver: Iberico Sweet Heat Dinner Sausage; Hot Honey.\n- Award of Excellence in 2011, 2013, 2015, 2019 and 2024.\n- Iberico-style line and a full-service custom-cut counter.\n- Charcuterie in select Metro, Sobeys, Foodland and Fortinos (Food Basics too, per the locations page).\n- 2 processing plants: Oshawa HQ at 915 Nelson St, and Etobicoke at 15 Brydon Dr, opened Aug 2020 with +200% capacity.\n- A Halenda's executive (David Hobson) joined the 2026 Meat & Poultry Ontario board.\n\nScale:\n- 5 Durham stores (Oshawa ×3, Whitby, Bowmanville) plus St Jacobs Market.\n- The fundraising program started June 27, 2013 and has sold 6M+ meat sticks. It claims \"more than six million dollars\" raised.\n- Implied run-rate (ESTIMATE): ≈ 1,880 cases or 45K bags a year, ≈ $450K a year to groups, and ≈ $0.5M a year of wholesale revenue for Halenda's.\n\nCounter-evidence: value-priced weekly specials and a single-SKU mass-market fundraiser product.",
        "pricing": "Fundraising Pepperoni Stix (verified Oct 16, 2026):\n- A case holds 240 x 50 g sticks (13 g protein each) in 24 bags of 10 (6 mild, 4 hot). Shelf life is 6 months, no refrigeration needed.\n- Groups sell at $25 a bag, so $600 a case at retail.\n- Case cost and share kept by the group:\n  - 1–7 cases: $300 (group keeps 50%).\n  - 8–14: $288 (52%).\n  - 15–24: $273 (54%).\n  - 25+: $261 (56%).\n- Shipping is $18 a case anywhere in Ontario, with no minimum. Pick-up is free in Oshawa or Etobicoke. No tax is charged. Lead time is about 2 weeks, up to 4 in peak season.\n- The same page still carries a stale PayPal block at $240 a case and $12 shipping.\n\nReal results:\n- A youth athlete sold 8 cases and raised $1,536 ($192 a case).\n- A dance studio raised $10,300.\n\n\"Hibernator\" freezer packs:\n- #1 at $199: pork chops, 2x16 oz sirloin, 4x8 oz striploin, 3 lb chicken breast, 2 lb bacon, 6 lb ground beef, 8 sausages. ESTIMATE: about 19 lb, ≈ $10.50/lb.\n- #2 at $299.\n- #3 at $349.\n\nOnline store (store.themeatdepot.ca, 75 SKUs, curbside pickup):\n- Chicken: boneless breast $7.99/lb; 11 lb bag $82.39 (≈ $7.49/lb).\n- Beef: lean ground $7.99/lb; AA+ striploin $12.49 per 10 oz (reg $16.24); sirloin $35.98 per 32 oz; PRIME T-bone $32.48.\n- Smoked meats: Kobassa $9.29/lb.\n- Prepared: perogies $4.99; cabbage rolls $2.99.\n- Specials Oct 15–21: pork souvlaki $4.99/lb; Ruby Red bacon $7.49/lb; Iberico stuffed pork loin $7.99/lb; porketta $1.99/100 g.\n\nJ&W comparison:\n- 55 lb boneless chicken breast is $350 (≈ $6.36/lb, about 15–20% cheaper).\n- 5 lb ground beef is $40 ($8.00/lb, at parity).\n- J&W has no meat stick of its own.",
        "scale": "- Stores: 5 retail stores, all in Durham:\n  - Whitby, 1916 Dundas St E.\n  - Oshawa Nelson HQ, 915 Nelson St.\n  - Oshawa King, 1300 King St E.\n  - Oshawa Taunton, 250 Taunton Rd E.\n  - Bowmanville, 94 Waverley Rd.\n- Plus St Jacobs Market (Woolwich) and 2 plants (Oshawa and Etobicoke).\n- Every store sits on J&W's Tuesday Durham route.\n- Family-owned since 1979.\n- Fundraising: 6M+ sticks since June 2013 and a claimed $6M+ raised for groups. Uses field sales reps (a required \"Sales Representative\" field on the order form).\n- Supermarket charcuterie listings.\n- Revenue, employee count, group count and review counts: not found.",
        "siteDesign": "Platform: WordPress \"Fusion\" theme across three domains.\n- halendasfundraising.com, by Lure Marketing.\n- halendas.com, by Beam Local.\n- store.themeatdepot.ca, by Eyebeam Design (WooCommerce).\n\nPalette (from CSS):\n- Brand orange #F58025 for primary buttons, the outlined header CTA and the round scroll-to-top disc. Hover #E2721B.\n- Text #000000.\n- Secondary button #3F3F3F.\n- Dark footer/UI #1D1D1D and #32373C.\n- Calculator panel in sky blue #56A0D3.\n- Otherwise white.\n\nTypography:\n- Raleway 600–700 for H1, H2, H4 and H5 and the all-caps letterspaced nav. Hero headlines are about 68px, centred and white.\n- Playfair Display 700 serif for H3 and H6 and hero sub-lines.\n- Open Sans 16px body.\n\nLayout:\n- White utility bar with social icons left and an outlined orange \"PURCHASE\" / \"What's on sale this week?\" button right.\n- Uppercase dropdown nav.\n- Full-bleed photo hero slider with a dark overlay and a centred white headline.\n- Centred single-column sections separated by short orange rules, with orange pill CTAs.\n- Purchase page: macro photo of a pile of pepperoni sticks, then a light-blue two-way profit-calculator card, a plain tier table and a numbered order block.\n- Mobile: centred logo plus hamburger.\n\nPhotography:\n- Real product macros (sticks, cured meats on wood boards) in warm natural light.\n- Moody food hero on the main site.\n- School-supplies imagery on the fundraising hero.\n- No athletes, teams or kids. That gap is an opening for J&W.\n\nTone: folksy, upbeat, exclamation-heavy and volunteer-practical.\n- \"less selling and less administration\".\n- \"No more melted chocolate\".\n- \"Warning Halenda's is habit forming!\".\n- \"Support Local, Support Canada!\".\n\nFor J&W's prototype, keep the clarity of the calculator and tier table. Add athlete and team photography, per-team pages with live progress bars, one domain and consistent current prices.",
        "keyFlows": [
          "Two-way profit calculator:\n1. The organiser enters a number of cases and sees the profit.\n2. Or they enter a dollar goal and click 'Get Estimate' to see the cases needed.\n3. A tier table below shows profit rising from 50% to 56% across 1–7, 8–14, 15–24 and 25+ cases.\nFor J&W, rebuild it as goal → orders, for example a $2,000 goal ≈ 100 Team Packs at a $20 donation each.",
          "Fundraiser order flow:\n1. Click 'Order Now' and pick a number of cases.\n2. Choose delivery ($18/case, anywhere in Ontario, no minimum) or free pick-up in Oshawa or Etobicoke.\n3. Pay by card, cheque or e-transfer.\n4. Halenda's contacts the group just before delivery with the details.\nThe order form asks for Group Name, Contact, Address, Coupon Code and a required Sales Representative. That is rep and coach attribution J&W should copy.",
          "Two campaign modes:\n1. 'Order the Stix, then sell' (about 95% of groups): the product is in hand, and a custom insert flyer personalises the bags.\n2. 'Collect orders, then buy' (about 5%): Halenda's emails a personalised flyer and order form (digital or print), and the group orders after collecting the money.\nJ&W equivalent: mode 2 becomes an online team page with a share link; mode 1 becomes a J&W hand-sell protein stick.",
          "Objection-handling and compliance block:\n- Meets Ontario school nutrition guidelines.\n- No MSG, trans fats or artificial sweeteners; allergen-free ingredients.\n- 6-month shelf life and no refrigeration ('No more melted chocolate').\n- A No-Contact Fundraising Protocol.\n- Full ingredient list shown.\nSchools need an equivalent FAQ before approving a J&W campaign.",
          "Segment success-story content engine: a new post every few weeks, one per segment (dance studio $10,300; youth BJJ athlete $1,536 from 8 cases in a month; hockey; cadets). Each ends with a 'Start Fundraising Today!' CTA, and they double as SEO pages.\nHalenda's also ran a 'Buy Canadian' post naming US-owned rivals (World's Finest, Krispy Kreme, Jack Link's, Little Caesars).",
          "Named freezer bundles: 'Hibernator' Packs #1, #2 and #3 at $199, $299 and $349, each with a full item list ('custom cut, freezer wrapped, guaranteed tender'). Order in store or online, with curbside pickup in 24–48 h.",
          "Weekly sale page and retention loop:\n- The page shows the sale window (Oct 15–21, 2026), then 'Only $X/lb – Save $Y/lb' cards with award badges.\n- Next comes a 'REVIEW US' button linking to Google.\n- An e-flyer signup captures postal code and 'location most frequented', which segments the list by store."
        ],
        "featuresToBorrow": [
          "Two-way fundraising calculator (goal → orders, orders → dollars raised) on the Team Fuel landing page",
          "Tiered group give-back that rises with volume (Halenda's: 50% → 56%). For J&W, for example 15% → 20% by order count, plus a bonus tier for groups with 25+ orders",
          "Two campaign modes: an online team storefront (pre-order, nothing paid upfront) and a hand-sell J&W protein stick or jerky case for groups that want product in hand",
          "Sales-rep or coach attribution field and coupon code on every order, so each sale is credited to the right team and rep",
          "School-compliance FAQ: Ontario school food guideline fit, ingredients, allergens, storage, no-contact handling",
          "Segment success-story posts with real dollars raised (hockey, soccer, dance, cadets, schools), used as SEO landing pages",
          "'Buy Ontario / Buy Canadian' positioning against US-owned fundraising brands, made stronger with J&W's Ontario-inspected Scarborough plant",
          "Named freezer bundles at three price points with full item lists. J&W's version could be Team Freezer Pack, Game-Week Meal Butler Pack and Family Hibernator",
          "Dated weekly specials page with 'Save $X/lb' and a review-request CTA",
          "Order-ahead and pick-up option at Brimley Rd alongside route delivery"
        ],
        "whatJWDoesBetter": [
          "It captures supporters as customers. Halenda's sells cases to the group, and the group resells bags for cash, so Halenda's never learns who the buyers are. A J&W Shopify team storefront makes every supporter a J&W account (with consent and Smile.io points), which is what makes fundraising an acquisition channel",
          "Bigger, more useful basket. Halenda's fundraises with one $25 bag of pork sticks; J&W can offer freezer packs, chicken-breast cases, chicken burgers and cooked Meal Butler week-packs that families already buy. ESTIMATE: a $120 order at 15% gives $18 per order, against $12.50 per Halenda's bag",
          "Cleaner nutrition story for sports parents. Meal Butler is salt- and sugar-free with no added oils, and the chicken is air-chilled, local, and raised without hormones or antibiotics. Halenda's sticks contain salt, sugar, dextrose and nitrate. (Caveat: a shelf-stable J&W stick would also need salt and cure, so market it as lower-sodium and clean-label, not salt-free)",
          "Pork-free choices. The Halenda's stick is pork; J&W chicken and beef options suit no-pork households across Scarborough and Durham. Do not claim halal broadly: only 5 SKUs mention it",
          "Home delivery already runs across Ontario: the Tuesday Durham route covers every Halenda's store town, plus long-haul routes and Toronto express. Halenda's online store is curbside-only, and its fundraising orders ship at $18 a case",
          "Cooked, portioned, à-la-carte meal prep (about 292 SKUs). Halenda's prepared foods are deli sides and party trays",
          "Cheaper bulk chicken: about $6.36/lb (55 lb case at $350) against Halenda's $7.49–$7.99/lb. Ground beef is at parity ($8.00 vs $7.99/lb)",
          "A single modern Shopify storefront with accounts, loyalty and gift cards. Halenda's runs three WordPress domains with conflicting prices on the same page",
          "Honest note: J&W is not better on plant credentials (both are inspected; Halenda's has two plants), awards (Halenda's has 3 Diamonds) or retail distribution. Heritage is roughly equal (1979 vs J&W's 1973/1995 family lineage)"
        ],
