        "correctedClaims": [
          "Program timeframe was 'not found' in the selection; it is now known. The first case shipped June 27, 2013, so the $6M+ is cumulative over about 13 years: roughly $450K a year to groups and about $0.5M a year of wholesale revenue for Halenda's (ESTIMATE). Group count is still not found",
          "Group give-back: Halenda's groups keep 50–56% of retail (they buy at $261–$300 a case and sell at $600). That is far above the 15–20% the selection assumes for J&W, so the J&W pitch must rest on no upfront cash, no product handling, a bigger basket, and products families already buy, or add a hand-sell stick at 40–50%",
          "Year-2 sizing of 100 groups × $4,000 ≈ $400K is close to Halenda's entire fundraising run-rate, which took 13 years and field reps to build. Treat it as a stretch; ≈ $0.25–0.45M a year by year 2 including DTC follow-on (ESTIMATE) is safer",
          "'Five GTA/Durham stores': all five are in Durham (Oshawa ×3, Whitby, Bowmanville). The Etobicoke site is a processing plant and fundraising pick-up point, not a store, even though the logo names Mississauga and Etobicoke",
          "'2026 Diamond Award plus six more': six products were awarded in total, and the Diamond winner (Stuffed Iberico Back Bacon) is one of the six (2 Platinum, 2 Gold, 2 Silver)",
          "Charcuterie retailers: the charcuterie page lists Metro, Sobeys, Foodland and Fortinos; Food Basics appears only on the locations page",
          "Halenda's own pages give conflicting figures: the About page says '$2,000,000 raised' and the Purchase page says '$6,000,000'; the '15 people raise $3,900' example predates current prices; and a legacy PayPal block shows $240 a case and $12 shipping. Cite only the current tier table",
          "'Premium': Halenda's quality is award-winning, but its positioning is value ('Quality, Service and Low Prices'; pork tenderloin $4.99/lb). It is a channel benchmark, not a luxury benchmark"
        ]
